<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>Section 3.5: Properties and Applications of Logarithms</title>
  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>

<p workspace="1in">
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <p><md>b = 10^a ~~~ \iff ~~~ \log_{10}(<fillin characters = "1" answer="b"/>) = <fillin characters="1" answer="a"/></md>
<md>\log_{10}(10^t) = <fillin characters = "1" answer="t"/></md>
<md>10^{\log_{10}(y)} = <fillin characters = "1"/></md></p>
    <p><md>b = e^a ~~~ \iff ~~~ \ln(<fillin characters = "1"/>) = <fillin characters = "1"/></md>
<md>\ln(e^t) = <fillin characters = "1"/></md>
<md>e^{\ln(y)} = <fillin characters = "1"/></md></p>
  </sidebyside>
  </p>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Property 1</title>
    <p>Multiplication inside logs.</p>
      
  </assemblage>
<p workspace="0.1in"/>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Property 2</title>
    <p>Division inside logs.</p>
  </assemblage>
<p workspace="0.1in"/>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Property 3</title>
    <p>Powers inside logs.</p>
  </assemblage>

  <p workspace="0.15in">* Use these properties to solve any exponential function, regardless of base!</p>
  <example>
    <statement>
    <sidebyside>
      <p workspace="1in">
        <md>4^t = 7</md>
      </p>
      <p workspace="1in">
        <md>7\cdot 3^{2t}+4 = 10</md>
      </p>
    </sidebyside>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S3-5-soln">
      <p>
        <m>4^t = 7</m>.  Since <m>t</m> is in the exponent and we are at 
        the point where it is #<m>^t = </m>#,  take the log of both sides since 
        logs get rid of exponents.  We'll use natural log almost exclusively 
        unless it is directly a power of 10.  Then we get to <md>{\ln(4^t) = \ln(7)}</md>
        and from here we can use property 3 to get the <m>t</m> out 
        of the exponent!  This leaves
        <md>
        t\ln(4)  = \ln(7).
        </md>
        Now divide both sides by <m>\ln(4)</m> and 
        <md>
          t = \dfrac{\ln(7)}{\ln(4)}
        </md>
        which is exact (<m>\approx 1.40368</m>).
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>7\cdot 3^{2t}+4 = 10</m>. isolate the exponential first, then take the natural log of
        both sides, just like the first example.
        <md>
          <mrow>7\cdot 3^{2t}+4 \amp = 10</mrow>
          <mrow>7\cdot 3^{2t} \amp = 6</mrow>
          <mrow>3^{2t} \amp = \dfrac{6}{7}</mrow>
          <mrow>\ln(3^{2t}) \amp = \ln(\dfrac{6}{7}) </mrow>
          <mrow>2t\ln(3) \amp = \ln\left(\dfrac{6}{7}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>t \amp = \dfrac{\ln(6/7)}{2\ln(3)}</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp  \approx -0.07016</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.5.2</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Solve each of the following equations <term>exactly</term> and then find an estimate that is accurate to 5 decimal places.</p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="a." cols="2">
          <li>
            <p workspace="1in"><m>3^t = 5</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p workspace="1in"><m>4\cdot 2^t-2=3</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p workspace="1in"><m>3.7\cdot(0.9)^{0.3t}+1.5 = 2.1</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p workspace="1in"><m>72 - 30(0.7)^{0.05t}=60</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p workspace="1in"><m>\ln(t)=-2</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p workspace="1in"><m>3+2\log_{10}(t)=3.5</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      </statement>

    <solution component="S3-5-soln">
      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow>3^t \amp = 5</mrow>
              <mrow>\ln(3^t) \amp = \ln(5)</mrow>
              <mrow>t\ln(3) \amp = \ln(5)</mrow>
              <mrow>t \amp = \dfrac{\ln(5)}{\ln(3)} \text{ (exact)}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp  \approx 1.46497  \text{ (approx)}</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow>4\cdot 2^t-2 \amp = 3</mrow>
              <mrow>4\cdot 2^t \amp = 5</mrow>
              <mrow>2^t \amp = \dfrac{5}{4}</mrow>
              <mrow>\ln(2^t) \amp = \ln(\dfrac{5}{4})</mrow>
              <mrow>t\ln(2) \amp = \ln(\dfrac{5}{4})</mrow>
              <mrow>t \amp = \dfrac{\ln(5/4)}{\ln(2)} \text{ (exact)} </mrow>
              <mrow> \amp \approx 0.32193</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow>3.7\cdot(0.9)^{0.3t}+1.5 \amp = 2.1</mrow>
              <mrow>3.7\cdot(0.9)^{0.3t} \amp = 0.6</mrow>
              <mrow>(0.9)^{0.3t} \amp = \dfrac{6}{37}</mrow>
              <mrow>\ln((0.9)^{0.3t}) \amp = \ln(\dfrac{6}{37})</mrow>
              <mrow>0.3t\ln(0.9) \amp = \ln\left(\dfrac{6}{37}\right)</mrow>
              <mrow>t \amp = \dfrac{\ln(6/37)}{0.3\ln(0.9)} \text{(exact)}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp \approx 57.55345</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow>72 - 30(0.7)^{0.05t} \amp = 60</mrow>
              <mrow>-30(0.7)^{0.05t} \amp = -12</mrow>
              <mrow>(0.7)^{0.05t} \amp = \dfrac{12}{30}</mrow>
              <mrow>\ln((0.7)^{0.05t}) \amp = \ln(\dfrac{12}{30})</mrow>
              <mrow>0.05t\ln((0.7)) \amp = \ln(\dfrac{12}{30})</mrow>
              <mrow>t \amp = \dfrac{\ln(2/5)}{0.05\ln(0.7)} \text{(exact)}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp \approx 51.37960 </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p> For this one, we use the relationship between <m>e^a = b</m>
            and <m>\ln(b)=a</m>:
            <md>
              <mrow>\ln(t) \amp = -2</mrow>
              <mrow>t \amp = e^{-2} \text{(exact)}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp \approx 0.13534</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>For this one, we use the relationship between <m>10^a = b</m>
            and <m>\log_{10}(b)=a</m>:
            <md>
              <mrow>3+2\log_{10}(t) \amp = 3.5</mrow>
              <mrow>2\log_{10}(t) \amp = 0.5</mrow>
              <mrow>\log_{10}(t) \amp = 0.25</mrow>
              <mrow>t \amp = 10^{0.25} \text{(exact)}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp \approx 1.77828</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </solution>
  </activity>


<assemblage>
    <title>The graph of <m>y=\ln(x)</m></title>
        <p>Graph <m>e^x</m> is shown above the line <m>y=x</m>, and <m>\ln(x)</m> is shown below the line <m>y=x</m>. Left is zoomed in, right is zoomed out.</p>

    <p>
  <sidebyside widths="28% 28% 40%">
    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        The graph e^x and ln(x) on the same plot.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-3-5a">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=e^t</definition>
          <definition>g(t)=ln(t)</definition>
          <definition>h(t)=t</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" />
            <graph function="f"/>
            <graph function="g" dash="4 4" stroke="red"/>
            <graph function="h" dash="10 10" stroke="black"/>
            <label anchor="(0.5,2.8)"><m>y=e^x</m></label>
            <label anchor="(1,-2.7)"><m>y=\ln(x)</m></label>
            <point p="(-1,f(-1))" alignment="w"><m>(-1,e^{-1})</m></point>
            <point p="(e^(-1),g(e^(-1)))" alignment="e"><m>(e^{-1},-1)</m></point>
            <point p="(0,f(0))" alignment="w"><m>(0,1)</m></point>
            <point p="(1,g(1))" alignment="e"><m>(1,0)</m></point>
            <point p="(1,f(1))" alignment="e"><m>(1,e)</m></point>
            <point p="(e^(1),g(e^(1)))" alignment="nw"><m>(e,1)</m></point>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        The graph e^x and ln(x) on the same plot.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-3-5b">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=e^t</definition>
          <definition>g(t)=ln(t)</definition>
          <definition>h(t)=t</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-15,-15,15,15)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" />
            <graph function="f"/>
            <graph function="g" dash="4 4" stroke="red"/>
            <graph function="h" dash="10 10" stroke="black"/>
            <label anchor="(5.5,13.8)"><m>y=e^x</m></label>
            <label anchor="(12,3.7)"><m>y=\ln(x)</m></label>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    <p>The graph of <m>y=\ln(x)</m>:
      <ul>
        <li><p>vertical asymptote at <m>x=0</m></p></li>
        <li><p>passes through <m>(1,0)</m></p></li>
        <li><p>is always increasing</p></li>
        <li><p>is always concave down</p></li>
        <li><p>increases without bound</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </sidebyside>

 </p>

    
  </assemblage>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.5.3</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>In the questions that follow, we compare and contrast the properties and behaviors of exponential and logarithmic functions.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0cm">
      <statement>
        <p>Go to Desmos and plot <m>f(x)=-e^{-(x-1)}+1</m>
 and <m>g(x) = \ln(x)</m>.
 Compare the properties of the two, noting what they have in common and what they don't.</p>
        <tabular>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>\quad f(x)=-e^{-(x-1)}+1 \quad </m></cell>
            <cell><m>\quad \quad g(x)=\ln(x) \quad \quad </m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">Domain:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">Range:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"><m>x</m>-intercept</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"><m>y</m>-intercept</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">increasing or decreasing:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">concave up or down:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">grow to <m>\infty</m> or bounded:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-5-soln">
        <tabular>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor"></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>\quad f(x)=-e^{-(x-1)}+1 \quad </m></cell>
            <cell><m>\quad g(x)=\ln(x) \quad </m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">Domain:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>(-\infty,\infty)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0,\infty)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">Range:</cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>(-\infty,1)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(-\infty,\infty)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"><m>x</m>-intercept</cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>(1,0)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1,0)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor"><m>y</m>-intercept</cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>(0,1-e)\approx(0,-1.718)</m></cell>
            <cell>DNE</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">increasing or decreasing:</cell>
            <cell right="minor">increasing</cell>
            <cell>increasing</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">concave up or down:</cell>
            <cell right="minor">concave down</cell>
            <cell>concave down</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">grow to <m>\infty</m> or bounded:</cell>
            <cell right="minor">bounded above by <m>1</m></cell>
            <cell>grows to <m>\infty</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          They have a few things in common and a few things different.  What 
          do you notice?
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0cm">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>h(t)=-ae^{-k(t-b)}+c</m>,
 where <m>a</m>,
 <m>b</m>,
 <m>c</m>,
 and <m>k</m>
 are positive constants. Describe <m>h</m>
 as a transformation of the function <m>y=e^{t}</m>.  (How does <m>c</m> transform <m>e^{t}</m>, ..., think shifts and stretches vertically or horizontally. Can use Desmos to help you)</p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>a</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>b</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>c</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>k</m>:</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-5-soln">
        <p>
          
          <ul>
            <li><p>
              <m>a</m>: a vertical stretch, the sign determines whether it flips over the x-axis
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              <m>b</m>: shifts left/right so a horizontal shift
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              <m>c</m>: shifts up/down so a vertical shift by <m>c</m>
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              <m>k</m>: a horizontal stretch, also affects increasing or decreasing 
              (if it flips over y-axis)
            </p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>

      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>r(t) = a\ln(t-b)+c</m>,
 where <m>a</m>,
 <m>b</m>,
 and <m>c</m>
 are positive constants. Describe <m>r</m>
 as a transformation of the function <m>y=\ln(t)</m>.  (Can use Desmos to help you.)</p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>a</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>b</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>c</m>:</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-5-soln">
        <p>
          Start from <m>y=\ln(t)</m>.
          <ul>
            <li><p>
              <m>a</m>: vertical stretch, affects increasing or decreasing (if it 
              flips over the x-axis or not)
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              <m>b</m>: horizontal shift left/right
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              <m>c</m>: vertical shift up/down
            </p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Data for the height of a tree is given in the table below; time 
 is measured in years and height of the tree is given in feet. At <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/0yy">gvsu.edu/s/0yy</url>, you can find a Desmos worksheet with this data already input.</p>
        <tabular>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell  right="minor">t</cell>
