            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>7</cell>
            <cell>8</cell>
            <cell>9</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>11</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell right="minor">h(t)</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>9.5</cell>
            <cell>13</cell>
            <cell>15</cell>
            <cell>16.5</cell>
            <cell>17.5</cell>
            <cell>18.5</cell>
            <cell>19</cell>
            <cell>19.5</cell>
            <cell>19.7</cell>
            <cell>19.8</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>Do you think this data is better modeled by a logarithmic function of form  <m>{p(t)=a\ln(t-b)+c}</m>
 or by an exponential function of form <m>{q(t)=me^{-rt}+n}</m>.
 Provide reasons based in how the data appears and how you think a tree grows, as well as by experimenting with sliders appropriately in Desmos. (Note: you may need to adjust the upper and lower bounds of several of the sliders in order to match the data well.)</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-5-soln">
        <p>
          Trees don't grow infinitely tall, they have some max height. So maybe 
          exponential is the <q>better</q> choice?
        </p>
        <p>
          We can play with the sliders.  Somewhere around the 
          following ish?
              <md>q(t) \approx -20.8e^{-0.35t}+20.3 \text{ (ish...)}</md>
        
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.5.4</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Solve each of the following equations for the exact value of the unknown variable (<m>k</m>).</p>
    <p>
      <ol marker="a." cols="2">
        <li>
          <p workspace="1.5in"><m>41 = 50e^{-7k}</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p workspace="1.5in"><m>65=34+47e^{-45k}</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p workspace="1.5in"><m>7e^{2k-1}+4=32</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p workspace="1.5in"><m>\frac{5}{1+2e^{-10k}}=4</m></p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    </statement>
 
    <solution component="S3-5-soln"><p>For the following problems, 
    use algebra to get the exponential alone first.  Then either use the relationship
    between exponential functions of base <m>e</m> and 
    natural log functions, or take the natural log of both side 
    and use the relationship of inverses, that <m>\ln(e^a) = a</m>.</p>
      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow>41 \amp = 50e^{-7k}</mrow>
              <mrow>\dfrac{41}{50} \amp = e^{-7k} </mrow>
              <mrow>\ln\left(\dfrac{41}{50}\right) \amp = -7k</mrow>
              <mrow>k \amp = \dfrac{\ln(41/50)}{-7} \text{ (exact)}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp  \approx 0.02835</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow>65 \amp = 34+47e^{-45k}</mrow>
              <mrow>31 \amp = 47e^{-45k}</mrow>
              <mrow> \dfrac{31}{47} \amp e^{-45k}</mrow>
               <mrow> \ln(\dfrac{31}{47}) \amp -45k</mrow>
              <mrow>k \amp = \dfrac{\ln(31/47)}{-45} \text{ (exact)}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp \approx 0.00925</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow>7e^{2k-1}+4 \amp = 32</mrow>
              <mrow>7e^{2k-1} \amp = 28</mrow>
              <mrow>e^{2k-1} \amp = 4</mrow>
              <mrow>2k-1 \amp = \ln(4)</mrow>
              <mrow>2k \amp =1+\ln(4) </mrow>
              <mrow>k \amp = \dfrac{1+\ln(4)}{2} \text{ (exact)}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp \approx 1.19315</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Here we need to do essentially the same thing, get the exponential by 
          itself first.  To do that, we need to get the whole denominator out of the denominator. 
          Since it's in the denominator it is being divided, so we can multiply 
          both sides by the entire denominator first.  On the left they will cancel.
            <md>
              <mrow>\dfrac{5}{1+2e^{-10k}} \amp = 4</mrow>
              <mrow>(1+2e^{-10k})\dfrac{5}{1+2e^{-10k}} \amp = 4(1+2e^{-10k})</mrow>
              <mrow>5 \amp = 4\left(1+2e^{-10k}\right)</mrow>
              <mrow>5 \amp = 4+8e^{-10k}</mrow>
              <mrow>1 \amp = 8e^{-10k}</mrow>
              <mrow>\frac18 \amp = e^{-10k}</mrow>
              <mrow>\ln(\dfrac{1}{8}) \amp = {-10k}</mrow>
              <mrow>k \amp = \dfrac{\ln(1/8)}{-10} \text{(exact)}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp \approx 0.20794</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Logs in the <q>wild.</q></title>
    <introduction>
      <p>When a population grows (decays) at a rate proportional to the current population, the population follows the model of exponential growth (decay): <md>P(t)=Ae^{kt}</md> where <m>P(t)</m> is the population at time <m>t</m>, <m>A</m> and <m>k</m> are constants.  If <m>k&gt;0</m> we are talking about growth (<m>P</m> is increasing), if <m>k&lt;0</m> we are talking about decay (<m>P</m> is decreasing).  Many populations grow/decay exponentially; one of the most common is bacteria.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Consider a population of bacteria <m>P(t)</m> where <m>P</m> is measure in millions of cells and <m>t</m> is in hours.  We know <m>P(0)=2.475</m>, and <m>P(10)=4.298</m>.  Find a model of the form <m>P(t)=Ae^{kt}</m> that fits this data by first plugging in <m>P(0)</m> to determine <m>A</m>, and then plugging in <m>P(10)</m> to determine <m>k</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-5-soln">
        <p>
          Since <m>P(0)=Ae^{0}=A</m>, and <m>P(0)=2.475</m> as well, we get <m>A=2.475</m>.  We can plug that 
          in so <m>P(t)=2.475e^{kt}</m>. Then we know <m>P(10)=4.298</m>, 
          so we can plug that in and simplify to get:
          <md>
            <mrow>4.298 \amp = 2.475e^{10k} </mrow>
            <mrow>\dfrac{4.298}{2.475}\amp e^{10k}</mrow>
            <mrow>\ln(\dfrac{4.298}{2.475})\amp {10k}</mrow>
            <mrow>k \amp = \dfrac{1}{10}\ln\left(\dfrac{4.298}{2.475}\right) \text{ (exact)}</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp \approx 0.05519</mrow>
          </md>
          So <m>P(t) = 2.475 e^{\dfrac{1}{10}\ln\left(\dfrac{4.298}{2.475}\right) t}</m> (or <m>P(t)\approx 2.475e^{0.05519t}</m>).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How many bacteria are there after 20 hours (<m>t=20</m>)?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-5-soln">
        <p>
          Plug in <m>t=20</m>, and we get <m>P(20)=2.475e^{\frac{1}{10}\ln\left(4.298/2.475\right) (20)}</m>,
          which is terrible but it turns out to be 
          approximately <m>7.464</m> million bacteria.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How long does it take the population of bacteria to reach 1 billion cells (1000 million cells)?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S3-5-soln">
        <p>No we are looking for <m>t</m> when <m>P(t) = 1000</m>, so we are solving the following for <m>t</m> (we'll leave 
        <m>k</m> here until the very end, but we know it's exact value as found in part 1):
          <md>
            <mrow>1000 \amp = 2.475e^{kt} </mrow>
            <mrow>\dfrac{1000}{2.475} \amp = e^{kt} </mrow>
            <mrow>\ln(\dfrac{1000}{2.475}) \amp = {kt} </mrow>
            <mrow>t \amp = \dfrac{\ln(1000/2.475)}{k} </mrow>
          </md>
          And now we can plug in <m>k</m>.  For the sake of not making it too complicated, 
          it is approximately <m>t\approx 108.741</m> hours (or about 4.5 days).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

</handout>
